{"id":"2109585456127971518","authorId":"2724095695","createdAt":"2026-10-12T10:02:21.000Z","type":"tweet","refId":null,"lang":"en","text":"Today and every day, we recognize and celebrate the cultures, histories, and contributions of Indigenous peoples.\n\nLong before these islands were called the Virgin Islands, they were home to the Ciboney, Taíno, and Carib (Kalinago) peoples, who fished our waters, farmed our land, and built thriving communities across St. Croix, St. Thomas, and St. John. Their legacy lives on in our landscape: in the petroglyphs carved at Reef Bay on St. John, in the ceremonial grounds uncovered at Cinnamon Bay, and at Salt River Bay on St. Croix, where Carib warriors stood their ground in 1493 in one of the first recorded acts of Indigenous resistance to European arrival in the Americas.\n\nOn Indigenous Peoples' Day, we honor these first Virgin Islanders, whose history is part of our history, and the Indigenous communities whose traditions have endured across generations and continue to shape our territory and our country.","capturedAt":"2026-10-12T11:31:27.615Z","metricsAtCapture":{"likes":20,"retweets":9,"replies":0,"quotes":0},"source":{"version":1,"url":"https://x.com/i/web/status/2109585456127971518","textField":"note_tweet.text","raw":{"text":"Today and every day, we recognize and celebrate the cultures, histories, and contributions of Indigenous peoples.\n\nLong before these islands were called the Virgin Islands, they were home to the Ciboney, Taíno, and Carib (Kalinago) peoples, who fished our waters, farmed our land, https://t.co/76alto4acZ","note_tweet":{"text":"Today and every day, we recognize and celebrate the cultures, histories, and contributions of Indigenous peoples.\n\nLong before these islands were called the Virgin Islands, they were home to the Ciboney, Taíno, and Carib (Kalinago) peoples, who fished our waters, farmed our land, and built thriving communities across St. Croix, St. Thomas, and St. John. Their legacy lives on in our landscape: in the petroglyphs carved at Reef Bay on St. John, in the ceremonial grounds uncovered at Cinnamon Bay, and at Salt River Bay on St. Croix, where Carib warriors stood their ground in 1493 in one of the first recorded acts of Indigenous resistance to European arrival in the Americas.\n\nOn Indigenous Peoples' Day, we honor these first Virgin Islanders, whose history is part of our history, and the Indigenous communities whose traditions have endured across generations and continue to shape our territory and our country."},"entities":{"urls":[{"display_url":"pic.x.com/76alto4acZ","end":304,"expanded_url":"https://x.com/StaceyPlaskett/status/2109585456127971518/photo/1","media_key":"3_2109585449274454017","start":281,"url":"https://t.co/76alto4acZ"}]},"created_at":"2026-10-12T10:02:21.000Z","conversation_id":"2109585456127971518","edit_history_tweet_ids":["2109585456127971518"]},"urls":[{"field":"entities","url":"https://t.co/76alto4acZ","expandedUrl":"https://x.com/StaceyPlaskett/status/2109585456127971518/photo/1","displayUrl":"pic.x.com/76alto4acZ","start":281,"end":304}],"references":[]}}
